  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nThe closed low situated over the western Great Lakes has not made \nmuch progress over the last several hours. This has helped to keep \nour area under a persistent pattern of low cloud cover through the \nafternoon, and will remain the case as the closed low slowly shifts \nnortheastward. With weak ascent in the lowest levels paired with \nsome residual low-level moisture, could see continued chances for \nsporadic, isolated mist/sprinkles through the rest of the day. As \nthe low lifts off towards the northeast, increasing entrainment of \ndrier air on the southwest edge of the cyclone will erode away at \nclouds, clearing out skies overnight tonight. Despite clearing skies \nand increasing subsidence with height rises behind the cyclone, \nwinds will remain just strong enough out of the west to prevent the \nnocturnal boundary layer from decoupling substantially. This will \nkeep temperatures within the low 40s across the area, and \nadditionally mitigate any fog development overnight.\n\nTomorrow, a cold front will drape southeastward from the retreating \nsurface cyclone, shifting winds out of the north/northwest and \nreintroducing low clouds to the area by the afternoon. With this \nfrontal boundary, some low (15-25%) chances for additional light \nrain exists across northern MO, where greater moisture remains \npresent through the vertical profile. However, with increasing \nsynoptic-scale subsidence on the back edge of the retreating trough \nand a building surface high during the day, the profile looks to \nquickly dry out. Temperatures remain in the 50s during the daytime, \nbefore winds shift out of the north with the oncoming surface high \nand cool temperatures down more by the end of the work week.\n\nAs amplified upper-level ridging establishes itself over the central \nCONUS, sustained northerly winds will provide strong CAA across the \nforecast area. The current forecast shows that temperatures will \nfall to the low 30s and even the high 20s Friday night into Saturday \nmorning. These temperatures coupled with light winds hints at \nfavorable conditions for frost/freeze, especially for northeastern \nKS and portions of northern/central MO. Probabilistic guidance \nfavors temperatures falling below freezing across these areas (40-\n60%) , with recent guidance trending colder. Forecast trends will \nneed to continue to be monitored, and the need for any headline \nissuances for frost/freeze may need to be considered in the upcoming \ndays, especially if these trends continue. \n\nBy the weekend, broad troughing over the southwestern CONUS \ngradually shifts eastward across the Gulf Coast, with low amplitude \nridging settling across the northern half of the CONUS. Increasingly \ndry atmospheric profiles with building ridging from the west will \nkeep precipitation out of the forecast, with temperatures beginning \nto warm into the 60s by the late weekend under clear skies.",
